extends includes/main.pug
block content

    // About
    section.about-section.extra-about-padding.text-center
        .inner-section
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.gx-4.gx-lg-5.justify-content-center
                    .col-12.about-container
                        img.img-fluid(src='assets/gallery/about/on-the-coast.jpg', alt='Me on the coast of Scotland')
                    .col-12.about-container.align-vertically
                        p.left-align After studying painting at art school, I initially worked in Community Arts before becoming an Art Psychotherapist. I worked for the NHS for many years using art materials to facilitate patients expressing, exploring, understanding, and ultimately improving their mental health before returning to painting full-time.
                        p.left-align In my paintings I am exploring my felt-experience of the sea, the sky, and the land in-between. My painting process is intuitive and is informed by what I call my 'heart response', my internal landscape's reflection to a remote external landscape, as opposed to a striving for a figurative or literal depiction. In my paintings I am aiming to capture an expansiveness, a limitlessness, a spaciousness or other worldliness, of the ephemeral quality of the ever-changing light and elemental energies. I am drawn to the sense of isolation and wonder one can experience when confronted by the immeasurable beauty and power of nature associated with the Romantic landscape painting tradition. I am based in Glasgow but spend a lot of time on the coast of Argyll, walking and absorbing this remote part of the Scottish coastline to later translate into painted pieces.
                        p.left-align More recently, I have moved from working on larger oil on canvas pieces to smaller mixed media pieces. I feel this more immediate approach is enabling me to continue to the explore light, colour and beauty of the landscape which I love but is allowing me to bring a different energy and vitality to my work. The above photo was taken at my favourite place overlooking the Sound of Jura from Knapdale, Argyll.